# Build output
/dist/
*.exe
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# The Linux/macOS client and server (their own release downloads, built by release.yml's
# unix-binaries job into stage/). They have NO file extension, so *.exe above does not cover
# them, and a local dry run of those steps would otherwise leave them untracked -- in
# packaging/release/'s case, inside a directory that IS tracked, one careless `git add -A`
# from being committed.
/stage/
/packaging/release/meshghost-linux-*
/packaging/release/meshghost-macos-*
/packaging/release/meshghost-server-linux-*
/packaging/release/meshghost-server-macos-*

# Exception: LuaSocket is a real vendored dependency for the Emerald BizHawk
# adapter (agent_docs/licensing.md, agent_docs/architecture.md's Phase 3
# ADR), not Go build output -- must actually be committed.
!/adapters/bizhawk/pokemon/emerald/lib/**/*.dll

# Probe/test logs. Every probe writes a timestamped log beside itself, deliberately (a verdict
# that lives only in the Lua Console has to be copied back by hand) -- but they are personal
# diagnostic output, they grow large, and once a run has been read its conclusions belong in
# agent_docs/verified.md, not in a megabyte of raw log. The PROBES themselves are kept and
# committed: they are the record of how each fact was established, and several have been reused
# to chase the same class of bug on a later ROM. 72 such logs (1.1MB) were untracked on
# 2026-08-18 when this rule replaced the two narrower ones it supersedes.
/adapters/bizhawk/pokemon/**/*.log

# The adapter script + lib/ under packaging/release/games/pokemon/emerald/ are assembled by
# .github/workflows/release.yml (copied fresh from adapters/bizhawk/pokemon/emerald/ each run) or by a
# local dry run -- never hand-edited, never committed (avoids a second copy of the adapter
# script drifting from the real one at adapters/bizhawk/pokemon/emerald/meshghost_emerald.lua).
# README.txt at this same path is NOT covered by these two patterns -- see the exception below;
# it's hand-written and committed, same as TEVI's/Pseudoregalia's games/<game>/README.txt.
/packaging/release/games/pokemon/emerald/meshghost_emerald.lua
/packaging/release/games/pokemon/emerald/lib/

# Same deal for Crystal: assembled by release.yml from adapters/bizhawk/pokemon/crystal/, never
# hand-edited, never committed. README.txt at that path IS committed, as with every other game.
/packaging/release/games/pokemon/crystal/meshghost_crystal.lua
/packaging/release/games/pokemon/crystal/lib/

# ap_try.flag makes the Crystal adapter run an Archipelago ROM with an address that has NOT been
# confirmed. It is a deliberate local experiment and must never be committed or shipped -- its
# presence in a release would silently lower the bar the refusal exists to hold. It was committed
# once by an over-broad "git add -A" on 2026-08-18 and removed the same day.
#
# Globbed, so the PARKED name is covered too. The flag is kept between experiments as
# `ap_try.flag.off` -- renaming it back is how you turn it on, which beats deleting it and
# re-creating it from memory later. Without the glob that parked file shows up untracked and the
# same over-broad `git add -A` sweeps it in, which is precisely how the live one got committed.
# release.yml still checks for the exact name `ap_try.flag`, so a parked one cannot fail a build.
/adapters/bizhawk/pokemon/crystal/ap_try.flag*

# Exception: the TEVI mod DLL is committed on purpose -- CI cannot build it (the
# .csproj needs the user's own proprietary game DLLs, which are never committed,
# per agent_docs/licensing.md), so this is our own compiled output, checked in
# deliberately. dev-scripts/build-tevi.bat produces it; release.yml's staleness
# gate fails the build if it's older than the source that produced it. Staged under a
# MeshGhost/ subfolder (not flat in games/tevi/) so the whole MeshGhost/ folder is a single
# drag-and-drop into BepInEx/plugins/ -- same reasoning as Pseudoregalia's drag-and-drop
# tree below, just one file instead of a whole runtime.
!/packaging/release/games/tevi/MeshGhost/MeshGhostTevi.dll

# Exception: the Pseudoregalia mod DLL is committed on purpose -- CI cannot build it (the
# CMake tree needs the private UEPseudo submodule and a locally installed UE4SS, see
# agent_docs/phases/phase7.md's 7.2 entry), so this is our own compiled output, checked in
# deliberately. dev-scripts/build-pseudoregalia.bat produces it; release.yml's staleness
# gate fails the build if it's older than the source that produced it.
!/packaging/release/games/pseudoregalia/pseudoregalia/Binaries/Win64/ue4ss/Mods/MeshGhostPseudo/dlls/main.dll

# Exception: the UE4SS runtime itself is bundled on purpose starting the v0.2.0 release --
# a deliberate exception to "user installs the modding tool themselves" (agent_docs/licensing.md's
# RE-UE4SS entry), since UE4SS is obscure enough that pointing users at a manual download hurt
# onboarding more than redistributing our own MIT-compliant build helps. Built from this repo's
# own pinned RE-UE4SS submodule via dev-scripts/stage-ue4ss-runtime.bat; release.yml's staleness
# gate fails the build if these drift from that submodule pin.
!/packaging/release/games/pseudoregalia/pseudoregalia/Binaries/Win64/dwmapi.dll
!/packaging/release/games/pseudoregalia/pseudoregalia/Binaries/Win64/ue4ss/UE4SS.dll

# Go
/vendor/

# .NET/C# adapter build output (e.g. adapters/tevi) -- generated files under obj/ embed
# absolute machine-specific paths (NuGet cache location under the building user's home
# directory), the exact kind of machine-identifying detail CLAUDE.md says never to commit.
bin/
obj/

# CMake build tree (e.g. adapters/pseudoregalia/MeshGhostPseudo/build/) -- generated
# .vcxproj/.cmake files plus every third-party dependency's own build output, thousands of
# files, none of it source. Rebuilt locally via cmake --build; never committed.
/adapters/pseudoregalia/MeshGhostPseudo/build/

# Dev-run logs (core.log/relay.log are stale names; meshghost.log/meshghost-server.log are
# what cmd/meshghost and cmd/meshghost-relay actually write today -- see main.go's
# openLogFile, one next to the exe wherever it's run from)
core.log
relay.log
meshghost.log
meshghost-server.log
# These append and rotate once at 1MiB (openLogFile in both mains), so the rotated
# generation needs ignoring too. Globbed rather than listed one name at a time: only
# meshghost.log.1 was listed, so a relay left running past 1MiB produced an untracked
# meshghost-server.log.1 that a `git add -A` would sweep straight in -- which is the exact
# accident the six -live logs below record.
meshghost.log*
meshghost-server.log*
core.log*
relay.log*
# Redirected stdout/stderr from scaffolding an agent starts for a live test (relay, core,
# fakeadapter). These are named per-run rather than by the binary, so the entries above do not
# catch them -- six of them were committed by accident on 2026-08-17 and removed again.
*-live.log
*-live.err.log
*-live.err2.log

# Backup-renamed binaries left behind when a running .exe couldn't be overwritten in place
# (e.g. go build racing a still-running process on Windows) -- found live 2026-08-14,
# meshghost.exe~/meshghost-relay.exe~ showed up untracked since *.exe above doesn't match the
# trailing ~.
*~

# OS cruft
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Editor/tooling local settings
.claude/settings.local.json

# Agent-local state under .claude/ -- per-machine scheduling/worktree/checkpoint bookkeeping
# written by the agent harness, never project content. These were covered ONLY by this clone's
# .git/info/exclude, which is local and never committed, so a fresh clone would have shown them
# as untracked and an over-broad `git add -A` could sweep them in -- exactly how ap_try.flag got
# committed once on 2026-08-18. Listed here so the protection travels with the repo.
# Deliberately enumerated rather than ignoring .claude/ wholesale, so that shared agent config
# (a committed skill or agent definition) stays committable if this repo ever grows one.
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock
.claude/scheduled_tasks.json
.claude/agent-registry.json
.claude/agent-memory-local/
.claude/assistant-daemon-state.json
.claude/first-run
.claude/routines/.state/
.claude/worktrees/
.claude/checkpoints/
.claude/mailbox/

# Per-machine BizHawk launchers with real local paths filled in (see
# dev-scripts/README.md) -- the tracked *.bat files stay generic/placeholder since this
# repo is public. Real reason this exists, not just a preference: a real two-peer Emerald
# test (2026-08-14) silently failed because instance 2 was launched by double-clicking
# EmuHawk directly, so MESHGHOST_BRIDGE_PORT was never set and both instances defaulted to
# the same bridge port -- a launcher that sets it for you removes that failure mode.
dev-scripts/*.local.bat

# Emulator saves/states (never commit ROMs or save data)
*.sav
*.state
*.srm
*.rom
*.gba

# Local dev/test process logs (relay/core started for a live session)
core-*.log
core-*.err.log
relay-*.log
relay-*.err.log

# A core reads config.json from its WORKING DIRECTORY, and under the dev loader that is
# dev-scripts/ -- so a config left here silently redirects a session's core to whatever relay it
# names. That cost a live debugging detour on 2026-08-19 (four peers dialling a dead relay port,
# looking like peers vanishing). Never commit one; delete strays.
dev-scripts/config.json

# BizHawk dev loader: its control file and log are per-session dev state, not project content.
# The wildcard covers the per-instance files a two-game session makes (MESHGHOST_DEV_LOADER_TARGET).
dev-scripts/bizhawk-dev-loader.target
dev-scripts/bizhawk-dev-loader*.target

# Screenshots the agent takes to debug with (dev-scripts/bizhawk-screenshot.lua), one folder
# per game so a session on one adapter cannot bury another. Same reasoning as the probe logs
# above: a working aid, never project content, and never evidence -- a picture is not proof of
# anything visual (agent_docs/testing.md).
/dev-scripts/shots/
/dev-scripts/*.png
/dev-scripts/*.log

# A core config dropped next to an adapter is a DEV artifact: the adapter-spawned core reads
# its working directory, so this is how you change -interp on a running session without
# relaunching the emulator. It must never ship -- the release config lives in packaging/.
adapters/**/config.json
